  Material-definition decision
    Retain shared BRDFProperties schema and C# models for existing material-wide properties.
    Per-axis-face overrides were withdrawn: texture-to-material mappings already let different
    block-face textures use different properties and naturally follow block rotation.

  ☐ Add optional depth-map tessellation and relief mapping through the production shader pipeline.

  ☐ Try and make tessellation do square subdivision by subdividing faces as quads.

  ☐ Implement scene reflections for PBR liquids.
    - Extend PBR.Liquids.md's current environment approximation with reflected scene radiance.
      Evaluate bounded screen-space reflection tracing against the available scene depth/color;
      specify which geometry is represented and provide an atmosphere/environment fallback for
      offscreen rays, disocclusions, missing depth and unsupported transparent receivers.
    - Use the animated liquid normal, view direction, material roughness and Fresnel response.
      Coordinate with refraction and total internal reflection so reflected/transmitted energy is
      combined once; replace the existing reflection approximation rather than adding it twice.
      Retain distinct material and emission behavior for water, lava and other liquids.
    - Share coherent scene snapshots with refraction where their publication boundaries permit it.
      Avoid active-framebuffer feedback, self-intersections and foreground leakage. Define handling
      of rough reflections, screen edges, underwater views and overlapping transparent surfaces.
    - Provide a persisted Liquid Reflections graphics option and bounded quality settings. When
      disabled, retain the inexpensive environment fallback and skip reflection-only work/resources;
      shared snapshots remain alive only while another enabled consumer needs them.
    - Reuse screen-resource lifecycle and shader ownership for resize, reload and runtime toggling.
      If temporal reconstruction is adopted, reject invalid history on camera/world changes and
      disocclusion. Support both PBR modes without requiring LumOn and coordinate color/exposure
      with the scene-linear HDR task.
    - Validate reflection direction, Fresnel weighting, roughness, fallback transitions, moving
      geometry, shorelines, submerged views and settings/lifecycle changes with focused subagent
      tests. Obtain user-run visual acceptance and measure GPU tracing, filtering, snapshot bandwidth
      and memory costs separately and with refraction enabled.

  ☐ Add a camera-contact waterline/meniscus screen effect at the liquid transition boundary.
    - Follow the active liquid-rendering task immediately. Simulate liquid contacting the camera
      lens when the view straddles the surface: a narrow curved/distorted waterline with restrained
      optical highlights and tint, rather than an abrupt above-water/underwater seam.
    - Derive the screen-space boundary from camera/liquid intersection and the animated local
      surface, not a fixed horizontal screen band or only the camera-underwater boolean. Handle
      partial submersion, camera pitch/roll, waves, flowing surfaces and entering/exiting liquid.
    - Integrate with existing liquid depth, fog and OIT ownership. Keep distortion bounded, avoid
      active-framebuffer feedback and prevent leakage onto unrelated foreground geometry or UI.
      Reuse refraction sampling/resources when available, with a defined fallback when disabled.
    - Keep this localized contact effect distinct from bulk underwater absorption/scattering and
      optional persistent lens droplets. Define suitable behavior for non-water liquids, including
      emissive liquids, instead of applying water highlights indiscriminately.
    - Expose a persisted graphics setting; disabling it must skip effect-specific rendering and
      resources. Support both PBR modes, runtime toggling, resize/reload and stable transitions
      without flicker when the camera or waves repeatedly cross the surface.
    - Validate boundary placement and depth rejection with focused subagent tests, then obtain
      user-run acceptance for slow/fast entry and exit, bobbing, tilted views, shorelines and waves.
      Measure GPU time and any scene-copy bandwidth; document the lens-contact approximation.

  ☐ Explore approximate underwater caustics and decide whether to adopt them.
    - Compare bounded procedural/projected approaches against desired appearance and GPU cost.
      Use water motion, light direction, receiver depth and surface orientation where appropriate.
    - Restrict contributions to submerged receiving surfaces under relevant water and illumination;
      avoid effects above water, through opaque occluders or where the sun is fully shadowed.
    - Evaluate both lighting pipelines, underwater transitions, temporal stability and double-counting
      with existing lighting. Clearly label the effect as an approximation, not traced light transport.
    - Record prototype results and an adoption decision before production implementation. If adopted,
      provide an independent toggle, strength/quality bounds and targeted visual/performance checks.

  ✘ Refactor PBR systems which currently directly use base-game renderbuffer attachments, to instead create a TextureView instance for those attachments and use the texture-view instead. @cancelled(26-10-02 12:33)
    - Verified prerequisite: installed engine primary/shadow attachments are texture-backed but use
      mutable TexImage2D storage. Real glTextureView objects require immutable storage; a headless
      diagnostic reproduced InvalidOperation with no view object created. Evidence:
      artifacts/EngineAttachmentViews/SetupDefaultFrameBuffers.il.txt and mutable-view-diagnostic.log.
    - Resolve whether to preserve engine allocation using non-owning GpuTexture wrappers or change
      engine attachment allocation to immutable storage for real OpenGL texture views.
    - Correct GpuTextureView creation validation: the diagnostic found IsValid reports true after
      rejected creation because it only checks a generated nonzero name.
    - Preserve engine storage ownership, sampling policy and attachment replacement/retirement;
      migrate direct lighting, composite/display resolve, liquids and water-volume inputs together.

